NOT VERIFIED BY THIS SECTION, and deliberately not inferred:
  * No run against a real model. The plan's DoD asks for a multi-slide deck built end to end
    against a live model; ws4c ships stub-agent coverage of the compiled graph only.
  * No CI observation. CI has never completed a run on ws4a or ws4b either, so ws4c's
    integration-graph job has never executed on a runner. Note the job was ALSO not gating
    anything until late in the build -- it sat in test-summary's `needs:` and echo block but not
    its failure loop, so a red suite exited 0. Fixed, and guarded by
    tests/unit/test_ci_test_summary_gates_every_job.py.
  * Everything here ran on SQLite. Two ws4c behaviours are uncharacterised on PostgreSQL:
    concurrent failing builders writing two chat messages in one superstep, and checkpoint
    growth (measured 538 KB across 14 rows for 31 slides with brand bytes EMPTY; ~8 MB per turn
    is realistic, `findings` is unbounded, and nothing calls `delete_thread`).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 3 — WHAT THIS FILE DOES *NOT* COVER
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Playwright specs are NOT in the pytest suite and so are not in any count above. ws4a widened the
CI e2e matrix from 23 to 36 collected specs and placed 13 in DELIBERATE_EXCLUSIONS; the current
state is recorded in `tests/unit/test_e2e_matrix_covers_specs.py`'s DELIBERATE_EXCLUSIONS, which
is the authority.  The 13 excluded specs fall into two distinct groups that must not be conflated:
  KIND A (6 specs) — documentation screenshot generators (01-generating-slides, 02-creating-profiles,
    03-advanced-configuration, 04-retrieving-feedback, 06-uploading-images, 07-exporting-to-google-
    slides).  These write PNG screenshots to docs/user-guide/images and are NEVER meant to run in CI.
  KIND B (7 specs) — quarantined pending re-authoring against the current AppLayout shell
    (save-points-versioning, share-link, slide-generator, routing, navigation, slide-host-frame,
    genie-detail-panel).  Pre-existing failures surfaced — not caused — by the matrix widening;
    each carries a FOLLOW-UP marker and is expected to return to the matrix once re-authored.
Of the 20 specs ws4a newly added (to matrix or KIND B — KIND A pre-existed outside the matrix),
13 pass (including `slide-viewer`) and 7 were quarantined as KIND B.

**Those 13 were measured LOCALLY, not in CI.** ws4a's branch was never pushed, so the widened
36-entry matrix has never executed on a runner. The local run used the installed Playwright
chromium against a dev server, once with and once without a live backend on 127.0.0.1:8000 — the
per-spec results were identical both ways, which is what established that the 7 KIND B failures are
environment-independent. Treat "13 pass" as a local measurement awaiting its first CI confirmation,
and do not cite this file as evidence that CI is green.

`tests/integration/` coverage is likewise a CI-wiring property, not a pytest count: all 17 files
are now named in a CI job (10 of them previously ran in no job at all). The guard is
`tests/unit/test_ci_collects_integration_tests.py`.

RE-DERIVE THIS BASELINE if your PR alters schemas, the ORM, or dependencies — ws4b, ws4c, ws4d and
ws4e all should assume they must. Those are exactly the changes that mutate a cause while
preserving a count.
